From c71df119483415a5a9e4650993cc66d9ab26ef96 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Joonhoe Kim <26rote@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 11:10:31 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 110/111] drm/msm/dpu: baldur: lower the refresh rate in the
 kernel when idle

Idle refresh used to be a user daemon (baldur-refreshd) that
switched between the 120/60/30 Hz modes. Every switch is a mode change
for mutter (monitors-changed), which closes GNOME's overview and app grid.

Do it inside the kernel instead: the committed mode stays 120 Hz and only
the vertical front porch of the running DSI video interface is stretched
(vtotal 3400 -> 6800 -> 13600 = 120 -> 60 -> 30 Hz). The DSI TOTAL
register and the INTF vsync period are rewritten (0065's update_vfp), then
the INTFs are flushed outside a commit under the CRTC's commit lock, so
userspace never sees a mode change. dpu_crtc_get_scanout_position() uses
the live vtotal so vblank timestamps stay right while stretched.

Policy (msm.idle_refresh_* parameters): 2 = auto (default): 1 s without
commits -> 60 Hz, 5 s -> 30 Hz; the next commit or an input event returns
to the mode. 1 = manual rate, 0 = off. The mode's vtotal is restored and
latched before system suspend and on encoder disable (a 30 Hz sleep once
ended in a TZ err-fatal).

Tested on the device: manual 120/60/30 changes
hit the rate on the INTF frame counter with a stable CRC and no errors,
and a person watching saw no flicker at any change; auto drops to 60 then
30 Hz and a commit or a key press brings back 120; with the overview open
it goes down to 30 Hz, the overview stays open and mutter emits no
MonitorsChanged; suspend at 30 Hz restores 3400 first and resumes fine.

Risks: the INTF flush outside a commit had never been used on this
hardware before (idle_refresh_selfflush=0 turns it off, so a change only lands
with the next commit); underruns cannot be counted while the encoder is in
RC idle; the first frame after a long idle can be up to ~33 ms late.
Not meant for upstream in this form (board-specific policy, one DSI encoder only).
